From 44b181d209979244736872e7cec927532f8adc4f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 16:42:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Give object and array controllers a working entity mode

NSObjectController had the accessors of Core Data's entity mode
(entityName, managedObjectContext, fetchPredicate) but none of the
behaviour: -fetchWithRequest:merge:error: was a stub returning NO, so
-fetch: did nothing; -newObject made an instance of the object class
whatever the entity; removing deleted nothing; managedObjectContext could
not be bound, so a xib's binding of it was refused with "No binding
exposed"; and a xib's entityName was not read. -initWithCoder: also asked
automaticallyPreparesContent before decoding it, so no controller loaded
from a nib or xib prepared its content.

They now do what macOS does, checked against AppKit on macOS 15:

- managedObjectContext and fetchPredicate are exposed bindings, with the
  binding names NSManagedObjectContextBinding and NSFetchPredicateBinding
  added; the controller now retains its context.
- -fetchWithRequest:merge:error: executes the request, or with nil the
  new -defaultFetchRequest (the entity and fetch predicate, and for an
  array controller its sort descriptors), and makes the result the
  content: an object controller's is the first object fetched, an array
  controller's all of them, added to what it has when merging. -fetch:
  does that on the next pass of the run loop, as macOS has since 10.5,
  and so does -setFetchPredicate: in entity mode.
- -newObject inserts a new object of the entity into the context.
- What -removeObject: and -removeObjects: remove (and so remove: and
  -removeObjectsAtArrangedObjectIndexes:) is deleted from the context,
  unless the content is bound, when the binding's owner decides.
- A controller decoded with automaticallyPreparesContent fetches when it
  has an entity, by then given its context by its binding, and otherwise
  prepares its content; an array controller's prepared content is an
  array holding one new object. The entity name is read from a xib's
  entityName as well as from a nib's _NSManagedProxy, a decoded controller
  gets NSMutableDictionary as object class unless one is given, as on
  macOS, and -entityName is added beside -entityNameKey.

gnustep-gui does not link Core Data, and AppKit has no hard dependency on
it either: NSFetchRequest and NSEntityDescription are looked up by name
at run time, and the context, request and objects are sent only the
messages Core Data defines for them.

Tests/gui/NSArrayController/entityMode.m stands in for Core Data with
classes of those names that keep objects in an array, and loads
EntityMode.xib, an array controller in entity mode with its context bound
to the file's owner and one in class mode that prepares its content. The
test does not build without the change, since NSManagedObjectContextBinding
is new; with the binding name spelt out it fails its first four checks and
then crashes. The same was checked against FreeCoreData.

This applies on top of "Arrange an array controller's objects as its
content changes", whose -removeObject: and -removeObjects: it extends.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
